{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every well-established project should have a philosophy that guides its development. Without a core philosophy, development can languish in endless decision-making and have weaker APIs as a result."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This document outlines the core principles that drive the development and feature-set of TanStack Form."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"upgrading-unified-apis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Upgrading unified APIs"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"APIs come with tradeoffs. As a result, it can be tempting to make each set of tradeoffs available to the user through different APIs. However, this can lead to a fragmented API that is harder to learn and use."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"While this may mean a higher learning curve, it means that you don't have to question which API to use internally or have higher cognitive overhead when switching between APIs."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"forms-need-flexibility","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Forms need flexibility"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Form is designed to be flexible and customizable. While many forms may conform to similar patterns, there are always exceptions; especially when forms are a core component of your application."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"As a result, TanStack Form supports multiple methods for validation:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Timing customizations"}]},{"type":"text","value":": You can validate on blur, change, submit, or even on mount."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Validation strategies"}]},{"type":"text","value":": You can validate on individual fields, the entire form, or a subset of fields."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom validation logic"}]},{"type":"text","value":": You can write your own validation logic or use a library like "},{"type":"link","href":"https://zod.dev/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Zod"}]},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"link","href":"https://valibot.dev/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Valibot"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom error messages"}]},{"type":"text","value":": You can customize the error messages for each field by returning any object from a validator."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Async validation"}]},{"type":"text","value":": You can validate fields asynchronously and have common utils like debouncing and cancellation handled for you."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"controlled-is-cool","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Controlled is Cool"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In a world where controlled vs. uncontrolled inputs are a hot topic, TanStack Form is firmly in the controlled camp."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This comes with a number of advantages:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Predictable"}]},{"type":"text","value":": You can predict the state of your form at any point in time."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Easier testing"}]},{"type":"text","value":": You can easily test your forms by passing in values and asserting on the output."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Non-DOM support"}]},{"type":"text","value":": You can use TanStack Form with React Native, Three.js framework adapters, or any other framework renderer."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Enhanced conditional logic"}]},{"type":"text","value":": You can easily conditionally show/hide fields based on the form state."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Debugging"}]},{"type":"text","value":": You can easily log the form state to the console to debug issues."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"libraries-are-liberating","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Libraries are liberating"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One of the main objectives of TanStack Form is that you should be wrapping it into your own component system or design system."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To support this, we have a number of utilities that make it easier to build your own components and customized hooks:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Exported from your own library with pre-bound components for your forms.\nexport const { useAppForm } = createFormHook(/* options */)","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Without doing so, you're adding substantially more boilerplate to your apps and making your forms less consistent and user-friendly."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"upgrading-unified-apis","text":"Upgrading unified APIs","level":2},{"id":"forms-need-flexibility","text":"Forms need flexibility","level":2},{"id":"controlled-is-cool","text":"Controlled is Cool","level":2},{"id":"libraries-are-liberating","text":"Libraries are liberating","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nEvery well-established project should have a philosophy that guides its development. Without a core philosophy, development can languish in endless decision-making and have weaker APIs as a result.\n\nThis document outlines the core principles that drive the development and feature-set of TanStack Form.\n\n## Upgrading unified APIs\n\nAPIs come with tradeoffs. As a result, it can be tempting to make each set of tradeoffs available to the user through different APIs. However, this can lead to a fragmented API that is harder to learn and use.\n\nWhile this may mean a higher learning curve, it means that you don't have to question which API to use internally or have higher cognitive overhead when switching between APIs.\n\n## Forms need flexibility\n\nTanStack Form is designed to be flexible and customizable. While many forms may conform to similar patterns, there are always exceptions; especially when forms are a core component of your application.\n\nAs a result, TanStack Form supports multiple methods for validation:\n\n- **Timing customizations**: You can validate on blur, change, submit, or even on mount.\n- **Validation strategies**: You can validate on individual fields, the entire form, or a subset of fields.\n- **Custom validation logic**: You can write your own validation logic or use a library like [Zod](https://zod.dev/) or [Valibot](https://valibot.dev/).\n- **Custom error messages**: You can customize the error messages for each field by returning any object from a validator.\n- **Async validation**: You can validate fields asynchronously and have common utils like debouncing and cancellation handled for you.\n\n## Controlled is Cool\n\nIn a world where controlled vs. uncontrolled inputs are a hot topic, TanStack Form is firmly in the controlled camp.\n\nThis comes with a number of advantages:\n\n- **Predictable**: You can predict the state of your form at any point in time.\n- **Easier testing**: You can easily test your forms by passing in values and asserting on the output.\n- **Non-DOM support**: You can use TanStack Form with React Native, Three.js framework adapters, or any other framework renderer.\n- **Enhanced conditional logic**: You can easily conditionally show/hide fields based on the form state.\n- **Debugging**: You can easily log the form state to the console to debug issues.\n\n## Libraries are liberating\n\nOne of the main objectives of TanStack Form is that you should be wrapping it into your own component system or design system.\n\nTo support this, we have a number of utilities that make it easier to build your own components and customized hooks:\n\n```ts\n// Exported from your own library with pre-bound components for your forms.\nexport const { useAppForm } = createFormHook(/* options */)\n```\n\nWithout doing so, you're adding substantially more boilerplate to your apps and making your forms less consistent and user-friendly.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: philosophy\ntitle: Philosophy\n---\n\nEvery well-established project should have a philosophy that guides its development. Without a core philosophy, development can languish in endless decision-making and have weaker APIs as a result.\n\nThis document outlines the core principles that drive the development and feature-set of TanStack Form.\n\n## Upgrading unified APIs\n\nAPIs come with tradeoffs. As a result, it can be tempting to make each set of tradeoffs available to the user through different APIs. However, this can lead to a fragmented API that is harder to learn and use.\n\nWhile this may mean a higher learning curve, it means that you don't have to question which API to use internally or have higher cognitive overhead when switching between APIs.\n\n## Forms need flexibility\n\nTanStack Form is designed to be flexible and customizable. While many forms may conform to similar patterns, there are always exceptions; especially when forms are a core component of your application.\n\nAs a result, TanStack Form supports multiple methods for validation:\n\n- **Timing customizations**: You can validate on blur, change, submit, or even on mount.\n- **Validation strategies**: You can validate on individual fields, the entire form, or a subset of fields.\n- **Custom validation logic**: You can write your own validation logic or use a library like [Zod](https://zod.dev/) or [Valibot](https://valibot.dev/).\n- **Custom error messages**: You can customize the error messages for each field by returning any object from a validator.\n- **Async validation**: You can validate fields asynchronously and have common utils like debouncing and cancellation handled for you.\n\n## Controlled is Cool\n\nIn a world where controlled vs. uncontrolled inputs are a hot topic, TanStack Form is firmly in the controlled camp.\n\nThis comes with a number of advantages:\n\n- **Predictable**: You can predict the state of your form at any point in time.\n- **Easier testing**: You can easily test your forms by passing in values and asserting on the output.\n- **Non-DOM support**: You can use TanStack Form with React Native, Three.js framework adapters, or any other framework renderer.\n- **Enhanced conditional logic**: You can easily conditionally show/hide fields based on the form state.\n- **Debugging**: You can easily log the form state to the console to debug issues.\n\n## Libraries are liberating\n\nOne of the main objectives of TanStack Form is that you should be wrapping it into your own component system or design system.\n\nTo support this, we have a number of utilities that make it easier to build your own components and customized hooks:\n\n```ts\n// Exported from your own library with pre-bound components for your forms.\nexport const { useAppForm } = createFormHook(/* options */)\n```\n\nWithout doing so, you're adding substantially more boilerplate to your apps and making your forms less consistent and user-friendly.\n"}
